## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CAPA design for data drift in a globally distributed system, emphasizing data quality governance, region-specific containment, and systematic problem resolution.

## Key Concepts
- Data drift detection and distribution monitoring across geographic regions
- CAPA data model with comprehensive evidence capture and drift scoring
- Region-aware lifecycle management with rollback capabilities
- Lightweight REST API for auditability and traceability
- Structured RCA methodology and controlled canary deployment

## Code Example
```javascript
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  region: string;
  evidence: string[];
  telemetry: Record<string, any>;
  driftScore: number;
  supplierVersion: string;
  status: 'detected' | 'containment' | 'resolution' | 'prevention';
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a structured CAPA for a real-world, beginner-friendly scenario with multi-region impact and a concrete data model.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data modeling for multi-region telemetry
- Simple lifecycle/state machine for containment
- Minimal REST API design to create/update CAPAs with evidence
- Region-aware metrics to prove containment
- RCA template and staged remediation (canary, quotas)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API sketch (Express)
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  // create CAPA skeleton
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'CAPA-001' });
});
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate CAPA effectiveness before closing it? 
- What data would you collect for RCA if the spike recurs in a new region?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical CAPA design for data segregation issues in multi-tenant exports, focusing on traceability and measurable containment.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model
- Lifecycle state machine
- Minimal REST API design
- Metrics to prove effectiveness
- RCA templates and canary-based remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express route to create CAPA
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
let caps = [];
app.post('/caps', (req,res)=>{ const cap = req.body; cap.id = cap.id || String(Date.now()); caps.push(cap); res.status(201).json(cap);});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you store and attach evidence files?
- How would you enforce tenant isolation in the API and data store?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model CAPA data, implement region-aware governance, and handle third-party feed drift with a real-world workflow, including evidence lineage, owners, and canary rollouts.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data model: incidentTime, vendor, feed, evidenceLinks, lineage, owners, actions, status, region
- Lifecycle: detect -> contain -> triage -> diagnose -> remediate -> verify -> rollback with region gates
- APIs: minimal CRUD (POST /caps, PUT /caps/{id}) including logs/traces and lineage
- Metrics: containment time by region/vendor, recurrence rate, false positives
- RCA & canary: structured root-cause sections; schema validation; provider failover

## Code Example

```typescript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  incidentTime: string;
  vendor: string;
  feed: string;
  evidenceLinks: string[];
  lineage: string;
  owners: string[];
  actions: string[];
  status: 'detect'|'contain'|'triage'|'diagnose'|'remediate'|'verify'|'rollback'|'closed';
  region: string;
}
```

